  "query_preview": "/Sunday through Wednesday/... \nDuring the first half of next week, the anti-cyclone center will \npropagate across the four corners into central Colorado allowing \nenhanced easterly flow and better chances of inverted shortwaves \nentering the SW Conus. However, there is considerable uncertainty \nregarding the timing and location of any particular feature which \nwould enhance thunderstorm chances, not to mention mesoscale \ndetails from decayed convective complexes which further \ncomplicates the forecast. However, as early as Saturday night, \nlong tracking, strong outflows may be sufficient to incur isolated\nstorm development over lower elevations, while deepening moisture\nprofiles, increasing total column PWATs, and setting the stage \nfor enhanced convective coverage on following days. Various \nmodeling suites highlight different days with a more preferred \nchance of storms sweeping into lower elevations early next week, \nbut all models indicate at least one easterly wave influencing \nenhanced activity. \n\nThis easterly flow will also help erode higher midlevel heights \nand temperatures; and combined with the convective influences, may\nsignificantly reduce and complicate heat related impacts. A \ngrowing trend in models shows outflows and convection rapidly \ncooling temperatures across Arizona early in the week such that by\nMonday, readings return to normal levels. GFS BUFR soundings are \neven more aggressive towards outflows affecting temperatures \nsuggesting extensive cloud cover much of the day Sunday, and \nreadings falling well short of 110F. Not putting too much stock in\nthese types of details 3-4 days in advance, but certainly the \npattern supports this evolution where somewhat lower temperatures \nand a return to only moderate HeatRisk arrives quicker than \npreviously forecast.",
